<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · British Isles</p><h1 class="st-title">East Breifne</h1><p class="st-meta">1256 CE – 1607 CE (approx.) · capital Cavan</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The O'Reilly lordship of County Cavan, a Gaelic state with a town at Cavan whose 'O'Reilly money' circulated in the Pale, until the Plantation of Ulster.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>East Breifne emerged when the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-breifne">Kingdom of Breifne</a> split in 1256 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-raghallaigh">O'Reillys</a>, Muintir Mhaoilmhordha, took the eastern part. They founded the town of Cavan beside their castle on Tullymongan hill and a Franciscan friary, and also held the island fortress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clough-oughter-castle">Clough Oughter</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/o-raghallaigh">O'Reillys</a> traded with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-pale">Pale</a>, and their 'O'Reilly money', clipped coins circulating in the 15th century, was common enough to be banned by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-ireland">Irish Parliament</a> in 1447. The lordship was organised in Gaelic fashion, with tanistry, brehon law and a hereditary learned class.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-tudor">Tudors</a> the area became County Cavan in 1584. After the Nine Years' War and the Flight of the Earls it was included in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a> from 1609, with lands granted to English and Scottish settlers.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/east-breifne.jpg" alt="East Breifne" width="320" height="400"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was (Automated conversion) at English Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ireland_1450.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1256 CE – 1607 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cavan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Irish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ireland">Ireland</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Breifne O Reilly, Muintir Mhaoilmhordha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1432&amp;focus=east-breifne" aria-label="East Breifne on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4519 327 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where East Breifne lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4839 583L4817 574M4839 583L4861 599"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4817 574h0M4861 599h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4839 583h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4839 583h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1432&amp;focus=east-breifne">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
